## 1. Introduction
Carbon dioxide $(\mathsf{C O}_{2})$ is a major greenhouse gas and it poses risks such as ocean acidification, glacier melting, and extreme weather events [1]. As the greatest source of anthropogenic $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ emissions, fossil fuel combustion represents $85\%$ of global energy production [2]. Thus, the liquidation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ emission has become a priority to achieve policy goals associated with the Paris Agreement [3,4]. However, with the growth of the global population and the increase in energy demand, it will not be easy to control atmospheric ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ emissions in the foreseeable future. Along with efforts to control ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ emission, it is also desirable to seek alternative routes for liquidation such as the fixation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ as a renewable feedstock (e.g., generating energy, materials, and fine chemicals) [4,5].
${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ can serve as a C1 building block for various organic chemicals [6] and industrial commodities [7]. However, chemical conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ involves a complex synthetic procedure as ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ is a very stable molecule. One of the most promising reaction schemes in this area is the formation of cyclic carbonates (e.g., coupling of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ and epoxides). In this context, the catalytic fixation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to generate five-membered alkylene carbonates (1,3-dioxolan-2- ones) is an appealing green chemical option as it does not generate adverse by-products [5]. Furthermore, as the end product of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ fixation, cyclic carbonates can be extensively employed for versatile applications including aprotic polar solvents, electrolytes in secondary batteries, intermediates (in pharmaceuticals), agricultural chemicals, and precursors for synthesizing polymeric materials [8].
To date, numerous catalyst systems have been built in homogeneous and heterogeneous modes to transform ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ into cyclic carbonates in association with metal complexes, organic bases or ammonium salts, and metal oxides [9]. In practice, homogeneous metal complexes, such as Al, Co, Mn, Zn, Cr, and Mg, were the first catalysts to convert ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ into cyclic carbonates under atmospheric pressure and ambient temperature [10,11]. However, many of these metal catalysts suffer from disadvantages like poor selectivity [10], low sensitivity to hydrolysis or oxidation [11], toxicity [12], the non-recyclability of the homogenous-catalysts [13], and difficulties in product separation [14].
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted broad research interest in the recent past due to their great catalytic potentials in energy and environmental applications. Using uniform pores within the frameworks, MOF-based materials can exhibit great potential as heterogeneous catalysts for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ -epoxide transformations [15]. Practically, MOF-based catalysts have several advantages for catalytic applications. First, the uniformity of the active catalytic sites results in high product selectivity over MOFs relative to other typical solid-supported catalysts. Second, as the porous MOFs can efficiently capture ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ at low pressure, they can promote the reaction at the ambient conditions [16]. Several MOFs have been used as catalysts for the cycloaddition of carbon dioxide to epoxides. Nonetheless, the scale-up of MOFs for the production of commodities is limited as they suffer from poor stability in the reaction media [17].
Ligands containing O and N donors have the great potential for the fine control of coordination architectures through facile coordination with metal centers [18]. Based on both theoretical and experimental investigations, the formed MOF can efficiently adsorb the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ molecules using the N/O-donor groups in the porous MOF structure [19]. Moreover, as the $\mathsf{N}/0$ atoms have greater electronegativity than C, their enhanced acidity can help promote the deprotonation during the reaction process [20]. In this work, an organic linker with high contents of $\mathsf{N}/0$ elements was employed for the synthesis of Co-based MOF catalyst with a nanoribbon structure and a large surface area to upgrade the performance of MOF in cycloaddition of carbon dioxide to epoxides. The prepared MOF recorded excellent yield and selectivity in the cycloaddition of carbon dioxide to epoxides to help establish a practical tool for the sustainable fixation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ for industrial commodities.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Chemicals
In the course of this research, the following chemicals were purchased from Aladdin Chemicals Co. Ltd. (Shanghai, China): 4,40-(4- amino-4H-1,2,4-triazole-3,5-diyl) dibenzoic acid $(\mathsf{C}_{16}\mathsf{H}_{12}\mathsf{N}_{4}\mathsf{O}_{4})$ , N, $N\mathrm{.}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF), cobalt(II) nitrate hexahydrate (Co $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}.6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , $99.9\%$ ) epichlorohydrin (ECH), propylene oxide (PO), styrene oxide (SO) and tetrabutylammonium bromide (TBAB), tetra- $\cdot n$ -butylammonium iodide (TBAI), potassium iodide (KI), deuterated chloroform $\mathrm{\CDCl}_{3}–\mathrm{d}$ ), methanol, dichloromethane (DCM), and acetone.
## 2.2. Preparation of $C o(I I)$ -MOF
The quasi-1D Co(II)-MOF nanoribbons were prepared by a facile solvothermal method. Briefly, $10\ \mathrm{mmol}$ of organic linker benzoic acid $(\mathsf{C}_{16}\mathsf{H}_{12}\mathsf{N}_{4}\mathsf{O}_{4})$ , abbreviated as BAAT, was dissolved in $70~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF via continuous stirring and ultra-sonication at room temperature. Then, 5 mmol of ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{N O}}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathsf{H}}_{2}0$ was added to the above solution. Afterward, the resulting mixture was transferred to a Teflonlined autoclave and was heated to $120^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ . The solid product was separated from the solvent using high-speed centrifugation $(10,000\mathrm{rpm})$ for $10\mathrm{min}$ and was washed repeatedly with acetone/ methanol (1:1). The final ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{I I}})$ -MOF powder had a pink color after freeze-drying. The coordinated polymer formed through successful complexion of cobalt $(\mathsf{C o}^{\mathrm{{II}+}})$ metal ions was identified in terms of the color difference between the bare ligand and Co(II)-MOF (Fig.S1).
## 2.3. Characterization
Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) analysis was conducted using $\mathtt{C u}\mathtt{K}\mathtt{\simeq}$ radiation $(30~\mathrm{kV}/160~\mathrm{mA}$ , $\lambda=1.54056\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ) at a scan rate of $0.05^{\circ}\ s^{-1}$ . The data were recorded in the 2h range of $5^{\circ}-65^{\circ}$ . $\mathrm{^1H}$ NMR and $^{13}{\mathsf{C}}$ NMR data were collected on a Bruker AVANCE-400 spectrometer. Chemical shifts are reported in d relative to TMS. The Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra (KBr disk, 4000– $400~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) were taken on a Bruker EQUINOX55 FT-IR spectrophotometer. The morphology, size, and structure were observed by scanning electron microscopy (JSM 6700) and transmission electron microscopy (JEM-2100F). The high-angle annular dark-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) image and EDS mapping images were taken on a JEOL JEM-ARF200F. Inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS) was performed on a PerkinElmer Optime 8000 ICP-AES/ICP-OES spectrometer.
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopic (XPS) measurements were taken on an X-ray photoelectron spectrometer (ESCALAB250,
## 2.4. Catalytic cycloaddition reaction of $C O_{2}$ and epoxide
The catalytic cycloaddition reaction was conducted in a stainless-steel autoclave $\mathrm{\Delta}_{\mathrm{\cdot}10\mathrm{\mL}},$ ) equipped with an adjustable auto magnetic stirring unit. In a typical reaction, epichlorohydrin (31 mmol), Co(II)-MOF (15 mg), and tetra-ntetrabutylammonium bromide (TBAB, $0.10\ \mathrm{~g},\ 0.3\ \mathrm{~mmol};$ were mixed together as epoxide, catalyst, and cocatalyst, respectively. This mixture was transferred to the autoclave after ultrasonication. Then, ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ was introduced into the container with an absolute pressure of 1 M Pa. The temperature was raised to $80~^{\circ}C$ under a constant stirring rate of $650~\mathrm{rpm}$ for durations of $1{-}3\mathrm{~h~}$ After the reaction process, the unreacted ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ gas was slowly vented out, and the catalyst was isolated by high-speed centrifugation. An aliquot of the resulting catalytic reaction mixture was taken and solubilized in $0.7~\mathrm{mL}$ $\mathsf{C D C l}_{3}$ -d and then analyzed by $\mathrm{^1H}$ NMR spectroscopy. For the recycling tests, the recovered ${\sf C o}({\sf I I})$ - MOF catalyst was repeatedly washed with $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{Cl}$ and dried at $60^{\circ}C$ in a vacuum before the subsequent use.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Synthetic mechanism for $C o(I I)$ -MOFs
The ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{I I}})$ -MOFs were synthesized by a one-pot solvothermal approach using $\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ as the metal source and BAAT as the organic linker. In Fig. 1a, the XRD patterns displays sharp peaks which indicates the good crystalline nature of the as-prepared Co (II)-MOFs [21,22]. This pattern couldn’t be attributed to any known Cobalt-based crystal structure, showing that we created a fully new material. Then, the BAAT was analyzed in reference to Co (II)-MOFs to identify the changes in its physical and/or chemical properties due to the coordination with the Co elements. According to the thermogravimetric analysis (TGA), displayed in Fig. 1b, the coordination of Co improved their thermal stability as the initial decomposition temperature of the BAAT significantly increased after the synthesis. Whereas, in the comparison of the UV–visible in Fig. 1c, the diffuse-reflectance spectra of the two samples notifies the appearance of a strong absorption peak around $\sim545~\mathrm{nm}$ in the ${\sf C o}({\sf I I})$ -MOF supports the successful coordination of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions into the BAAT [23]. In Fig. 1d, the FTIR spectroscopy showed that most of the functional groups in BAAT have been preserved after the coordination with $C0^{2+}$ ions, while the peaks associated with ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{-}}0{\mathsf{-}}{\mathsf{C}}$ and $\mathsf{N{\mathrm{-}}H}$ groups (e.g., at 1120 and $3158~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) disappeared [22]. This suggests that the ${\mathsf{C o}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ ions broke the $\mathsf{N}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{H}$ bonds and $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}0{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}$ bonds in TAAB after the solvothermal reaction to form Co-N and Co-O bonds, which also led to the enhancement of $c{\mathrm{-}}c$ related bonds in the range of $1400{-}1600~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The above observations indicate that Co atoms should have been genuinely coordinated with the N and/or O atoms to form crystalline-structured MOFs.
XPS showed the presence of four prominent peaks, corresponding to cobalt (Co 2p), oxygen (O 1 s), nitrogen (N 1 s), and carbon (C 1 s) in the range of 200–800 eV (Fig. 1e). The atomic percentage of each element, as summarized in Table S1, indicates that the concentrations of N, O, and Co metal ions were 13.7, 23.0, and $5.3\ \mathrm{at}\%$ . According to the narrow scan of the N1s and O1s peak (Fig. S2), the existence of Co-N and Co-O bonds is apparent beside the other bonds ascribable to the BAAT precursor. This can be regarded as direct evidence of the Co coordination with the $0/\mathsf{N}$ elements in the BAAT molecules [24,25]. The Co 2p peak analysis indicates the charge state of the Co atoms (Fig. 1f). The Co 2p region is separated into two distinct peaks at 781.5 and $797.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ , corresponding to Co $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ and Co $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ respectively. The binding energy separation between these two peaks is $15.9\mathrm{eV}$ , which is different from the situation of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ species [26]. In the meantime, the appearance of a satellite peak around $\sim786$ eV (due to the high spin states ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ ) also verified the $^{+2}$ charge state of the Co.
XPS characterizations have shown that ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ ions coordinate with the O and N atoms on the BAAT molecules. 1,2,3-triazoles, and 1,2,4-triazole-based ligands were also used to form polymeric coordination networks where protonated $^{1,2,4}$ -triazole can function as a dinucleating ligand $\mathrm{~\textmu~}1,2-$ and $\upmu\ 2,4-$ binding modes) while deprotonated 1,2,4-triazolate can coordinate two or three metal ions [18,19]. Based on this information, we draw in Fig. $_{1\mathrm{g}}$ probable coordination positions of $C0^{2+}$ ions on the BAAT molecules. Note that the BAAT molecules have a linear structure, while the oxygen-containing groups are at the edge of the linear structure. As a result, the as-formed MOFs may display a onedimensional structure with high contents of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{\bar{\mathbb{I}}}$ atoms distributed on the surfaces of the MOFs.
## 3.2. Morphologic characterization of $C o(I I)$ -MOFs
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) have been used to characterize the morphology and microstructure of the as-synthesized samples, respectively. From the SEM image (Fig. 2a), the as-synthesized Co(II)-MOF shows a ribbon structure with a $200{-}500{-}\mathrm{nm}$ width. TEM images indicate that each ribbon was composed of smaller ribbons with an average width of $60\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 2b-e). These small ribbons assemble into smooth arrays with a length of several micrometers. From the obtained high-resolution TEM image of Co(II)-MOF nanoribbons, it can be clearly seen that the sample has a nano-scaled porous structure without the segregation of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ metal ions to form metal clusters or nanoparticles (Fig. 2f). The combination of highangle annular dark-field images and the corresponding EDS mapping of each element in the Co(II)-MOF sample indicates the existence of Co, N, C, and O elements and their genuine distribution. Based on the EDS (Fig. S3) and inductively coupled plasma mass spectroscopic (ICP-MS) analysis, the atomic content of the Co element was about $5\pm0.2\%$ in the whole sample.
## 3.3. $C O_{2}$ adsorption experiment of $C o(I I)$ -MOF nanoribbons
$\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms at $77~\mathrm{K}$ were obtained to quantify the porous structure of the Co(II)-MOF nanoribbons (Fig. 3a). A typical type $\mathsf{I V}$ isotherm associated with H4 hysteresis loops was observed at a relative pressure $\left(\mathrm{P/P_{0}}\right)$ of 0.4–0.95. The consistent increase in adsorption of ${\sf N}_{2}$ molecules implies the presence of abundant micro-meso-macrostructures [27,28]. The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area and average pore diameter of the ${\mathrm{Co}}({\mathrm{II}}).$ -MOF nanoribbons were $46.8~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\bar{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ and $1.7\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. This surface area is very promising since another previously reported as prepared Co-based catalyst showed a value of $12\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ [29]. The ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ gas adsorption isotherm of ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{I I}})$ -MOF nanoribbons was measured at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ under atmospheric pressure (Fig. 3b). The steadily ascending patterns of slope indicate strong adsorption of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ molecules on $C o(\mathrm{II})\cdot$ - MOF nanoribbons [29,30]. The ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption capacity was measured to be $1.59\mathrm{\mmol}\mathrm{\g}^{-1}$ with a partition coefficient (PC) of $15.9~\upmu\mathrm{mol}~\upk g^{-1}~\mathsf{P a}^{-1}$ , which is among the highest observed for
MOF materials (Table 1) under specified conditions. The high concentration ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ uptake should be ascribed to the following aspects. First, there are abundant pores in the Co(II)-MOF nanoribbons to accommodate the large volume of gases observed in the ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms. Secondly, the unsaturated ${\sf C o}({\sf I I})$ metal ions and allocated $\mathsf{N}$ sites show good affinity for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ molecules, which can increase their stability in the MOFs [19,31]. The efficient ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture by the ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathrm{II}}).$ - MOFs should greatly favor the catalytic fixation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ into cyclic carbonates during the reaction.
## 3.4. Evaluation of catalytic performance towards cycloaddition of $C O_{2}$ with epoxides
The catalytic performance of the as-synthesized ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{I I}})$ -MOF nanoribbons was evaluated by conducting cycloaddition of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ with epichlorohydrin (ECH) epoxide as a model reaction under solvent-free conditions (Fig. 4a). To optimize the reaction condition, different types of cocatalysts (such as TBAB, TBAI, and KI) were first used to check their feasibility under specified reaction conditions as described in Table 2. Compared with the pure Co (II)-MOF nanoribbons (as a catalyst), the combination of ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathrm{II}}).$ - MOF nanoribbons and cocatalysts (TBAB, TBAI, and KI) have greatly enhanced the yield of chloropropene carbonate (CC) in all the cases, as listed in Table 2. The highest yield $(97\%)$ of chloropropene carbonate was achieved with a reaction rate of $14.6\times10^{6}\upmu{\mathrm{mol}}\mathrm{g}^{-}$ $^1\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ over $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ based on the $\mathrm{^1H}$ NMR results when the TBAB was used as a cocatalyst (Fig. 4b).
To further enhance the performance of the ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{I I}})$ -MOF nanoribbons/TBAB catalyst for the cycloaddition of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ with epichlorohydrin, the effects of process variables (the reaction time, temperature and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ pressure) were investigated in a series of experiments (Fig. 5a-c). It was demonstrated that the maximum conversion of $97\%$ was obtained within $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ with a selectivity of $>99\%$ (Fig. 5a-b) at $\mathrm{T}=80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathrm{P}_{\mathrm{CO}2}=1$ MPa as well as a selectivity of $99\%$ . However, as the temperature rises (up to $110^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ and beyond), there was a noticeable decline in the selectivity of the target product with the destruction of the active sites. By increasing the pressure of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ from 1 atm to $1\mathrm{MPa}$ , the yield of cyclic carbonate gradually increased to a value of $97\%$ due to the increased solubility of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ in the epichlorohydrin (Fig. 5c). The catalytic performance of cobalt-based MOF nanoribbons for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ fixation has been compared with those reported previously in terms of the type of catalysts, morphology, yield, and reaction rate under specified conditions (e.g., temperature and the pressure of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ ) (Table 3). Table 3 shows that the Co(II)-MOF nanoribbons investigated in our study exhibited excellent performance in terms of yield and final reaction kinetics compared to all other studies used for comparison. Its excellent catalytic performance (e.g., $99\%$ reaction selectivity and $97\%$ yield) can be attributed to the abundant nanopores in the synthesized MOFs and strong chemical interactions of oxygen and nitrogen atoms with the adsorbed ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . At the same time, the fact that we achieved the highest kinetic rate (i.e., $14.6\times10^{6}\mathrm{\upmumol\mathrm{g}}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1})$ compared to other studies (Table 3) can be attributed to the large number of Co (II) catalytic sites on the surface that ultimately facilitated the maximum catalytic reaction for the conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to epoxides. The slight decrease in the product yield as the cycle increases is likely due to the loss of catalyst during the washing process. In addition, the ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathrm{II}}).$ - MOF nanoribbons also showed stable high activity and selectivity during the repeated cycling process (Fig. 5d), supporting their utility in the conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to valuable epoxides.
3.5. Catalytic mechanism for cycloaddition of $C O_{2}$ with epichlorohydrin by $C o(I I)$ -MOF nanoribbons
To understand the good catalytic performance of ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{I I}})$ -MOF nanoribbons/TBAB, the catalytic mechanism was analyzed based on the reported literature [31,32]. This mechanism generally involves three main steps: epoxide ring opening, $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ insertion, and ring closure to form the five-membered cyclic carbonate. A probable epoxidation reaction process of epichlorohydrin and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ by ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{I I}})$ -MOF nanoribbons is as follows (Fig. 6):
(i) The epoxide molecule is adsorbed on the Lewis acidic Co(II) center due to the strong interactions between the oxygen atoms in the epoxide ring.   
(ii) The $\mathsf{B r}^{-}$ ion from the TBAB attacks the less-hindered carbon atom in the epoxide ring, resulting in its opening.   
(iii) The ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ molecule adsorbed in the nanoribbon interacts with the oxygen of the opened epoxy ring to generate an alkyl carbonate anion.   
(iv) Due to the electron transfer, the dangling oxygen atom will interact with the dangling carbon atom to form a closed ring, resulting in the corresponding cyclic carbonate.   
(v) Finally, the product is released from the catalytic site.
Based on the mechanism analysis, the number of ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{I I}})$ sites on the surface is critical for the reaction to proceed. The cocatalyst plays an important role in opening the epoxide ring, and the adsorption towards the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ molecules is also important for the whole reaction. According to the material characterization, the ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{I I}})$ -MOF nanoribbons present a large population of Co(II) atoms with high activity on the surface and show good ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ molecules uptake due to the high content of N and O in the MOFs. On the other hand, the good stability should be ascribed to the nonsolvent reaction condition, which does not destroy the structure of the catalysts even at a reaction temperature of $80~^{\circ}C$ and 1 MPa gas pressure. Based on the TEM and FTIR results of the fresh and recycled catalysts, the nanoribbon structure and the functional groups on the materials have been well preserved, confirming its good stability during the reaction (Fig. S4 and S5).
## 3.6. Investigation of the substrate scope
To prove the universality of ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{I I}})$ -MOF nanoribbons as catalysts for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ chemical fixation, two other epoxide substrates were used to react with the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ under the optimized reaction conditions $B0^{\circ}C,$ , 3 h and 1 MPa ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ pressure) with TBAB as the cocatalyst (Table 4). Based on the $\mathrm{^1H}$ NMR and $^{13}{\mathsf{C}}$ NMR analysis results, the yields of corresponding cyclic carbonates were $98\%$ with a selectivity of $299.9\%$ using 1,2-epoxypropane as a substrate. When the styrene oxide was used as the substrate, the yield of styrene carbonate was $85\%$ (Fig. S6-S11). The lower conversion efficiency for the styrene oxide than the epichlorohydrin and 1,2- epoxypropane should be due to the steric hindrance from the aromatic ring. These yields were among the best values under similar conditions as compared with the other catalysts for these two reactions. The findings indicate that the ${\sf C o}({\sf I I})$ -MOF nanoribbons were efficient catalysts for cycloaddition reaction with ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ of different epoxide substrates.
## 4. Conclusions
A ${\sf C o}({\sf I I})$ -based MOF nanoribbon was prepared by coordinating ${\mathsf{C o}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ ions with a new ligand $(\mathsf{C}_{16}\mathsf{H}_{12}\mathsf{N}_{4}\mathsf{O}_{4})$ that has abundant oxygen and nitrogen moieties to promote the chemical conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to corresponding epoxides. The superior performance of the synthesized one-dimensional ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{I I}})$ -MOF can be explained by the synergistic action between the high concentration of $0/\mathsf{N}$ species and the co-catalyst that enhance the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ uptake capacity and selectivity. Further, the combination of our synthesized Co(II)-MOF catalyst with TBAB as a co-catalyst exhibited excellent catalytic activity in terms of the reaction kinetics $\mathit{^{\prime}14.68\times10^{6}\mu m o l\mathrm{\mug}^{-}}$ ${}^{1}\ensuremath{\mathrm{h}}^{-1}.$ ), selectivity $(>99\%)$ , and yield $(97\%)$ towards the cycloaddition of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ with various epoxides under solvent free conditions. In addition to the higher activity of synthesized ${\mathsf{C o}}(\mathrm{II})$ -MOF catalyst, it also demonstrated excellent cycling stability towards the cycloaddition reaction due to the avoidance of acid and base reaction media to achieve the respective ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion to epoxides. This work is expected to help provide a strategy to design suitable MOF materials with unique morphologies and compositions for highly efficient ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ fixation and its conversion into valuable products based on the rational selection of suitable ligands.
